  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Light southerly flow in advance of an \napproaching cold front will help hold dewpoints in the mid to \nupper 70s through this afternoon. While there is some \nuncertainty regarding the placement of the front, and when \nconvection gets going, it appears there will be an opportunity \nfor sufficient heating south of HWY 70, supporting highs in the \nlow to mid 90s and heat indices of 105-107 degrees. In light of \nthis, a small Heat Advisory has been issued for late this \nmorning into this afternoon. Along the Crystal Coast, heat \nindices may hold around 105 degrees through early this evening. \nNorth of the coast, an increased risk of convection and clouds \nshould begin to lower the heat risk a bit earlier (ie. 3-5pm \ninland vs 7-8pm along the coast).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Over the next couple of days, it appears that \nvarious mesoscale features will heavily dictate where the risk \nof severe thunderstorms and flooding will be focused. In light \nof that, it's important to keep in mind that some fluctuations \nin the risk areas are probable as the convective evolution \nbecomes more clear.\n\nMesoscale features aside, the pattern checks several boxes for \nheavy rain rates, flooding, and severe weather. A high PWAT \nairmass is already in place, and will remain so through Saturday\nwith values of 2-2.25\" forecast. Meanwhile, heating of a very \nmoist boundary layer should yield moderate to strong instability\nboth today and Saturday. Today, in particular, MLCAPE is \nforecast to reach at least 2000- 3000j/kg, with some areas \npotentially exceeding 3000j/kg. Deep layer shear is forecast to \nbe marginal, but may reach 20-25kt near a front that is forecast\nto stall across the area today. That should support a somewhat \nimproved potential for storm organization. Consequently, the \ncombination of strong instability and modest shear should \nsupport the potential for some rotating updrafts capable of hail\nup to around quarter size. Strong updrafts and enhanced \nstretching in the vicinity of various mesoscale boundaries may \nalso support a brief/weak tornado risk. The most likely threat, \nthough, is damaging, waterloaded downdrafts of 40-60 mph. \nLastly, the combination of strong instability and an anomalously\nmoist airmass should support the potential of 2-3\"/hr rainfall \nrates. While thunderstorms are likely to propagate along various\noutflows and mesoscale boundaries, those rates should support a\nrisk of flooding of urban and poor drainage areas, with an \nassociated risk for flash flooding. This risk looks to be the \nhighest in some of the more urbanized areas from Jacksonville to\nNew Bern and north through Kinston and Greenville. \n\nNow the complicated part. Guidance differ on the placement of \nthe cold front and various outflow boundaries noted on radar and\nsatellite this morning. The general consensus appears to be for\nthe cold front to stall from near Kenansville east to near New \nBern. Today's forecast and messaging is heavily dependent on \nthat location. For example, there should be a maximum in low-\nlevel convergence where the seabreeze interacts with the front, \nand is where rainfall rates and amounts could potentially reach \n2-5\". If the front ends up further north, the heavy rain \nfootprint would follow suite, and vice-versa. To try to best \ncapture the various scenarios, a targeted Flood Watch has been \nissued covering the Greenville to Jacksonville to New Bern \ncorridor where the risk of flooding appears to be the highest. \nOf note, the immediate Crystal Coast was left out as that area \nmay tend to be in the shadow of the seabreeze, with lower \nrainfall totals. North and east of the watch area, instability \nand forcing may not be as high, which should also keep rainfall \nrates and amounts lower. Stay tuned as we continue to refine the\nareas at greatest risk for severe weather and flooding.",
